-- title:
--   Bounded denominators of an ODE solution are geometric: $M^{k+1}a_k\in\mathbb{Z}$
-- statement:
--   Let $n\ge 0$, let $g\in\mathbb{Q}(z,y_0,\dots,y_{n-1})$ be a rational function in $n+1$ variables, and let $f=\sum_{k\ge 0}a_kz^k\in\mathbb{Q}[[z]]$ be a formal power series that solves the algebraic differential equation
--
--   $$f^{(n)}(z)=g\bigl(z,f(z),f'(z),\dots,f^{(n-1)}(z)\bigr)$$
--
--   at a non-singular point, meaning that $g$ is defined at $\bigl(0,f(0),f'(0),\dots,f^{(n-1)}(0)\bigr)$.
--
--   Suppose that $f$ has bounded denominators in the sense of condition (2) of the Lam–Litt conjecture: there is $N\in\mathbb{N}$ with $a_k\in\mathbb{Z}[1/N]$ for all $k$. Then there is an integer $M\ge 1$ such that
--
--   $$M^{k+1}a_k\in\mathbb{Z}\qquad\text{for all }k\ge 0.$$
--
--   Equivalently, the rescaled series $M\,f(Mz)=\sum_{k\ge 0}M^{k+1}a_kz^k$ has integer coefficients.
--
--   Condition (2) only says that finitely many primes occur in the denominators of the $a_k$; it puts no bound on the exponents of these primes. For a solution of a non-singular algebraic differential equation the exponents grow at most linearly in $k$, so that $f$ becomes integral after the substitution $z\mapsto Mz$ (compare the integrality requirement $d\,f(cz)\in\mathbb{Z}[[z]]$ in Christol's notion of a globally bounded power series). This is the step that reduces implication (2)$\Rightarrow$(1) of the Lam–Litt conjecture to power series with integer coefficients.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The hypothesis on $f$ and $g$ is the mission's predicate `IsSolutionOfAlgebraicODE`: $g=p/q$ for polynomials $p,q$ with $q\bigl(0,f(0),\dots,f^{(n-1)}(0)\bigr)\neq 0$ and $f^{(n)}\cdot q(z,f,\dots,f^{(n-1)})=p(z,f,\dots,f^{(n-1)})$ in $\mathbb{Q}[[z]]$. The order $n=0$ is allowed; then $f$ is a rational function without pole at $0$. The ring $\mathbb{Z}[1/N]$ is the subalgebra of $\mathbb{Q}$ generated by $1/N$; since $1/0=0$ in Lean, $N=0$ gives $\mathbb{Z}$. The conclusion is stated as: there is a natural number $M>0$ such that every $M^{k+1}a_k$ is the image of an integer.
-- source:
--   Reduction step for implication (2) => (1) of Conjecture 1.1.1 of Y. H. J. Lam, D. Litt, Algebraicity and integrality of solutions to differential equations, https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.13175 (v1), p. 3 (condition (2), integrality), which is Problem 1 of D. Litt, https://www.problemsilike.com/1 . The statement is not in the paper; it follows from the factorial denominator bound for solutions of non-singular algebraic differential equations (theorem LamLitt.factorial_mul_coeff_denominator_bound on this platform) and Legendre's formula for the p-adic valuation of k!. For the notion of globally bounded power series see G. Christol, Globally bounded solutions of differential equations, in Analytic Number Theory, Lecture Notes in Math. 1434, Springer, https://doi.org/10.1007/BFb0097124 .

import Definitions.Def_LamLitt_Defs
import Mathlib

open PowerSeries

namespace LamLitt

theorem coeff_denominator_bound_of_integrality {n : ℕ} (f : PowerSeries ℚ)
    (g : MvRatFunc (Fin (n + 1)) ℚ) (hODE : IsSolutionOfAlgebraicODE n f g)
    (N : ℕ) (hN : IsCoeffIntegralAdjointInvNat f N) :
    ∃ M : ℕ, 0 < M ∧ ∀ k : ℕ, ∃ m : ℤ,
      (M : ℚ) ^ (k + 1) * PowerSeries.coeff k f = (m : ℚ) := by sorry

end LamLitt
